---
description: Travelar repository-first full-stack implementation and PostgreSQL safety
alwaysApply: true
---

Before substantial work, read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/engineering/implementation-directive.md`, `docs/engineering/decisions/ADR-064-postgresql-baseline.md`, `docs/engineering/postgresql-connection-and-docker.md`, and relevant requirements/addenda.

Treat the repository as an existing codebase to audit, not a blank starter. Preserve useful work; inspect frontend, backend, database, migrations, auth, integrations, tests and deployment; produce a requirement-to-code gap matrix before large changes.

PostgreSQL is the selected database baseline. Do not force a major upgrade; inspect the actual server and environment first. IDE connectivity alone does not prove app runtime connectivity or deployment configuration. Start with read-only inspection. No writes to an unidentified database. No production migrations/deployment, destructive SQL, live payments/refunds, permission/security changes or provider activation without explicit human approval.

Docker is optional. Prefer a safe, isolated dedicated test database where available. Recommend Docker/Compose only when reproducibility, disposable state, pinned versions, CI or dependent services justify it. Never point destructive test cleanup at production or shared data.

Implement the requirements pack across backend, database, authorization, UI, integrations, tests, operations and documentation. A polished screen is not completion evidence. Record commands, exit codes, test environment, migration impact/rollback, security findings, remaining gaps and exact evidence.
